(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-nervous-tissue-cbf-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What paired arteries supply blood to the brain?","choices":["Internal carotid and vertebral arteries","Only the internal carotid arteries","Only the vertebral arteries","External carotid and subclavian arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"The brain receives blood from the paired internal carotid and vertebral arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where does the internal carotid artery (ICA) branch from, and how does it enter the skull?","choices":["Arises as a branch of the subclavian artery, entering the skull through the foramen magnum","Arises directly as a branch of the aorta, entering the skull through the jugular foramen","Branches from the common carotid artery in the neck, entering through the carotid canal","Arises as a branch of the vertebral artery, entering the skull through the optic canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal carotid artery (ICA) branches from the common carotid artery in the neck and enters the skull through the carotid canal. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where do the vertebral arteries branch from, and what path do they take to enter the skull?","choices":["Arise directly as branches of the aorta, passing through the jugular foramen into the skull","Arise as branches of the common carotid artery, passing through the carotid canal into the skull","Branch from the subclavian artery, passing through the transverse foramina of C1-C6 and through the foramen magnum","Branch from the internal carotid artery, passing through the optic canal into the orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vertebral arteries branch from the subclavian artery and pass within the transverse foramina of C1-C6, entering the skull through the foramen magnum. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the cerebral arterial circle (Circle of Willis), and what four paired arteries make it up?","choices":["Made up only of the paired vertebral arteries themselves, with no contribution whatsoever from the internal carotid or communicating vessels of the anterior circulation","A collateral circulation structure made up of paired anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral arteries, plus an unpaired anterior communicating branch","A venous structure draining the brain, formed from the confluence of the cerebral veins into the dural sinuses before reaching the internal jugular veins","A single unpaired artery supplying only the occipital lobe of the cerebrum and nothing else, with no collateral connections to other vessels"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cerebral arterial circle (Circle of Willis) provides collateral circulation and is made up of four pairs of arteries — anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral — plus an unpaired anterior communicating branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the function of the anterior communicating artery within the Circle of Willis?","choices":["An unpaired branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels","A vein draining the cavernous sinus into the jugular system","The main artery supplying the medulla oblongata directly","A paired artery supplying the cerebellum directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior communicating artery is an unpaired branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels, completing the anterior portion of the cerebral arterial circle. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the arterial pathway from the aorta to the cerebral arterial circle via the anterior route?","choices":["Aorta, then the subclavian artery, then the vertebral arteries, then the basilar artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle","Aorta, then the common carotid artery, then the external carotid artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle","Aorta → brachiocephalic trunk → common carotid → internal carotid arteries → cerebral arterial circle","Aorta → internal jugular vein → cerebral arterial circle directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior arterial route runs from the aorta, through the brachiocephalic trunk, to the common carotid, to the internal carotid arteries, and into the cerebral arterial circle. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the arterial pathway from the aorta to the cerebral arterial circle via the posterior route?","choices":["Aorta → common carotid → internal carotid → cerebral arterial circle directly, bypassing the vertebral system entirely","Aorta → external carotid → basilar artery → cerebral arterial circle, skipping the subclavian and vertebral vessels","Aorta → brachiocephalic trunk → subclavian artery → vertebral arteries → fuse to form the basilar artery → posterior cerebral/posterior communicating arteries → internal carotid arteries","Aorta, then the internal jugular vein, then the basilar artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle, a purely venous-to-arterial route"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior arterial route runs from the aorta, through the brachiocephalic trunk (on the right), to the subclavian artery, to the vertebral arteries, which fuse to form the basilar artery (branching in the area of the pons), continuing to the posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries, which connect to the internal carotid arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are dural venous sinuses, and where do they lie?","choices":["Lymphatic channels that drain fluid away from the brain into the neck","Arteries running through the subarachnoid space around the brain","Channels between the periosteal and meningeal layers of dura, into which cerebral/cerebellar veins drain","Channels located within the pia mater only, close to the surface of the brain tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dural venous sinuses are channels between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the dura mater, into which veins of the brain (cerebral veins, cerebellar veins) drain. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Where does blood in the dural venous sinuses typically flow toward?","choices":["The internal carotid artery","The vertebral artery","The pulmonary artery","The internal jugular vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blood in the dural venous sinuses typically flows toward the internal jugular vein. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which major sinuses are located within the falx cerebri?","choices":["Sigmoid and transverse sinuses, located posteriorly and laterally near the skull base","Only the cavernous sinus itself, near the sella turcica","Superior sagittal, inferior sagittal, and straight sinuses","Cavernous and petrosal sinuses, located near the base of the skull"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior sagittal, inferior sagittal, and straight sinuses are major sinuses located within the falx cerebri. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What makes the cavernous sinuses unique, in terms of their relationship to cranial nerves and arteries?","choices":["The cavernous sinus contains no cranial nerve or arterial relationships at all, being an isolated venous space","No cranial nerves whatsoever are located anywhere near the cavernous sinus along its entire course through the skull","Only CN VI is located in the cavernous sinus, with no arterial relationship present at all in this region","CN III, IV, V1, and V2 are located within the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus; CN VI and the internal carotid artery pass through the center of the sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cavernous sinuses are unique due to their close relationship to multiple cranial nerves and the internal carotid arteries: CN III, IV, V1, and V2 are located within the lateral wall, while CN VI and the internal carotid artery pass through the center of the sinus. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What do paired superior petrosal and transverse sinuses funnel blood into?","choices":["The straight sinus","The sigmoid sinuses","The cavernous sinuses","The superior sagittal sinus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Paired superior petrosal and transverse sinuses funnel blood into the sigmoid sinuses, which are continuous with the internal jugular veins. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are sigmoid sinuses continuous with?","choices":["The internal carotid arteries","The vertebral arteries","The internal jugular veins","The superior vena cava directly, bypassing the jugular veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Paired sigmoid sinuses are continuous with the internal jugular veins. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"According to the material's summary, where does all venous blood from the cranium eventually lead?","choices":["The pulmonary vein","The hepatic portal vein","The Superior Vena Cava","The Inferior Vena Cava"],"answer":2,"exp":"\"All roads lead to the Superior Vena Cava\" — venous return from the cranium ultimately drains to the superior vena cava. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the straight sinus drain?","choices":["The spinal cord exclusively via the epidural venous plexus","Only the orbit and surrounding eye structures","The internal cerebral veins and great cerebral vein","Only the cavernous sinus itself, exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"The straight sinus drains the internal cerebral veins and the great cerebral vein. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What do the cavernous sinuses drain to, and what do they receive venous blood from?","choices":["Drain into the superior sagittal sinus; receive venous blood exclusively from the cerebellum","Drain directly into the superior vena cava; receive venous blood exclusively from the orbit","Drain into the straight sinus; receive venous blood exclusively from the spinal cord","Drain to the petrosal sinuses; receive venous blood from the orbit as well as the brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cavernous sinuses drain to the petrosal sinuses and receive venous blood from the orbit as well as the brain. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where do left and right venous sinuses converge, and where does this converged point drain to?","choices":["Converge at the transverse sinuses at the confluence of sinuses; this drains to the sigmoid sinus, then to the internal jugular veins","Converge in the subarachnoid space; this drains onward to the choroid plexus for reabsorption","Converge specifically at the cavernous sinus, which then drains directly into the vertebral artery","Converge at the straight sinus only; this single sinus then drains directly onward into the aorta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Venous sinuses converge at the left and right transverse sinuses at the confluence of sinuses, which drains to the sigmoid sinus, and from there to the internal jugular veins, and eventually the superior vena cava. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which artery is described as branching many times in the area of the pons after the vertebral arteries fuse to form it?","choices":["The posterior communicating artery","The basilar artery","The anterior cerebral artery","The internal carotid artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The basilar artery, formed by the fusion of the vertebral arteries, branches many times in the area of the pons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which two arteries connect the basilar artery and the internal carotid arteries, per the material?","choices":["The superior and inferior cerebellar arteries","The middle cerebral and ophthalmic arteries","Posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries","The anterior cerebral and anterior communicating arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries connect the basilar artery and the internal carotid arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What structure in a representative neuron diagram represents the initial segment of the axon?","choices":["The region just distal to the axon hillock, where the axon begins before becoming myelinated","The terminal end of the axon located at the synapse itself","The specific region where the dendrites first emerge and branch from the cell body","The nucleus of the neuron, located within its cell body region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The initial segment of the axon is the region just distal to the axon hillock, where the axon begins (before it may become myelinated further along its length). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is the Golgi apparatus located within a representative neuron, per the material's labeled diagram?","choices":["Within the axon terminals at the distal synaptic ending only","Outside the neuron entirely, within the surrounding neuroglia","Embedded within the layered myelin sheath surrounding the axon","Within the cell body, among the other organelles like Nissl bodies and mitochondria"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Golgi apparatus is located within the cell body, alongside other organelles such as Nissl bodies, mitochondria, the nucleus, and nucleolus, per the material's labeled diagram of a representative neuron. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do astrocyte projections anchor to, forming the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["Capillaries","The dura mater directly","Peripheral nerve axons","The ependyma lining the ventricles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Astrocytes are star-shaped cells with projections that anchor to capillaries, forming the blood-brain barrier, which isolates the CNS from the general circulation. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What type of cells line fluid-filled passageways within the brain and spinal cord?","choices":["The astrocytes, a star-shaped glial cell type","The microglia, a phagocytic glial cell type","The oligodendrocytes, a myelin-forming glial cell type","Ependymal cells (simple cuboidal epithelial cells)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ependymal cells, which are simple cuboidal epithelial cells, line the fluid-filled passageways within the brain and spinal cord. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Besides the posterior cerebral artery, which other named arteries branch from the basilar artery to supply the cerebellum and nearby structures, per the labeled diagram?","choices":["Only the middle cerebral artery, a branch of the internal carotid","Only the anterior cerebral artery, a branch of the internal carotid","Only the anterior communicating artery, connecting anterior vessels","Superior cerebellar, pontine, labyrinthine, and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The basilar artery gives rise to branches including the superior cerebellar, pontine, labyrinthine, and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries, in addition to the posterior cerebral arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which artery is described as arising near the vertebral arteries to supply the posterior inferior cerebellum and adjacent spinal cord region?","choices":["Posterior inferior cerebellar artery, alongside the anterior spinal artery","The middle cerebral artery, from the internal carotid","The anterior communicating artery, joining anterior vessels","The ophthalmic artery, which instead supplies the orbit and surrounding eye structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery arises near the vertebral arteries (alongside the anterior spinal artery) to supply the posterior inferior cerebellum and adjacent spinal cord region. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which sinus is listed alongside the confluence of sinuses in the venous drainage diagram, receiving drainage near the occipital region?","choices":["The occipital sinus","The petrosal sinus only","The cavernous sinus","The superior sagittal sinus only, with no occipital contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"The occipital sinus is listed in the venous drainage diagram, contributing near the confluence of sinuses at the back of the skull. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What do the superior and inferior cerebral veins drain into, per the venous drainage diagram?","choices":["The subarachnoid space only, with no further downstream vessel","Directly into the internal carotid artery lumen","The choroid plexus of the ventricles","The superior sagittal sinus and other adjacent dural sinuses"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior and inferior cerebral veins (roots of superior cerebral veins, inferior cerebrals) drain into the superior sagittal sinus and other adjacent dural sinuses, per the venous drainage diagram. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid artery, supplying much of the lateral cerebral surface?","choices":["Posterior communicating artery","Middle cerebral artery","Ophthalmic artery","Anterior communicating artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The middle cerebral artery is a major branch of the internal carotid artery, supplying much of the lateral cerebral surface. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the anterior spinal artery, arising near the vertebral arteries, supply?","choices":["The middle ear","The spinal cord","The orbit","The lateral cerebral surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior spinal artery, arising near the vertebral arteries, supplies the spinal cord. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"At the confluence of sinuses, which sinuses converge before draining onward to the transverse sinuses?","choices":["The cavernous sinus and superior petrosal sinus, forming a separate pathway","The sigmoid sinus and internal carotid artery together","The superior sagittal sinus, straight sinus, and occipital sinus","The internal jugular vein and vertebral artery together"],"answer":2,"exp":"At the confluence of sinuses, the superior sagittal sinus, straight sinus, and occipital sinus converge before draining onward to the (left and right) transverse sinuses. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 31"}]};
